   with any copy that still carried the tombstone (a phone that had not synced since the removal; an export
   taken that morning) took the number off again, silently, and a driver was handed a sheet without it. The
   absence of a tombstone is not a restoration, exactly as the absence of a value was not a deletion. So a
   removal and a putting-back are BOTH written down, each with its time, and neither is ever rubbed out: the
   later of the two is the one that stands, on every copy, however the copies meet. A tie stands as removed —
   a number that should not be there is worse on a drop sheet than a number missing from one. */
const UNDO = 'back/';
function untomb(id, who){ if (!id) return; S.deleted = S.deleted || {}; S.deleted[UNDO + id] = afterStamp(S.deleted[id]);
  const w = String(who == null ? (S.operator || '') : who).trim(); if (w) { S.by = S.by || {}; S.by['deleted/' + UNDO + id] = w; } }
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ set aside, with a way back
   The list collections — costs, variances, fencing dockets, breakdowns, actions, added assets — used to be
   REMOVED by splicing the record out of its list and leaving a tombstone. The tombstone kept the removal from
   coming back through a merge, which was right; but the record itself was gone, so there was no way back,
   and "remove" quietly meant "delete". Andrew Fisher, 16 Sep 2026: nothing is ever deleted, only set aside
   with a way back.

   setAside(coll, id, who) moves the record out of its list into S.aside under its id, whole, with who and
   when, and tombstones the id exactly as before — so every list, total, export and merge keeps leaving it
   out and nothing downstream has to learn a new rule. putBack(id, who) is the way back: the tombstone's
