   allAssets() 6,692 times, and every call rebuilt the whole projection from the schedule, the added rows, the
   accessories, what has been set aside and what has moved — 28.9 million inner lookups between them. The list is
   a DERIVATION: the same record gives the same list. It cannot change while one synchronous pass is running,
   because nothing else can run during one.

   SO THE HOLD LASTS EXACTLY ONE PASS, AND NOT ONE INSTRUCTION LONGER. It is put in place at the top of a pass
   and taken away in a `finally` at the bottom, so an error mid-draw cannot leave a stale list behind. Outside a
   pass every caller builds it fresh, exactly as before. There is no cache keyed on a version, nothing to
   invalidate, and nothing that can be quietly wrong later — the only window in which the held list is used is
   one in which the record provably cannot have moved. */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ v5.59 — WHAT IS INSIDE A BUILDING, ONCE
   Andrew Fisher, 19 Sep 2026: every building's fridges and air conditioners had already been supplied, yet some
   were missing. He had. It was in two drawers with two shapes: on P01, P03, P04 and P05 the
   fridge and two air conditioners were recorded as UNITS (a name, an asset number); on P09, P18, P19, P20 and
   P33 the same kinds of thing were recorded as ACCESSORIES. The accessories list — the one the drawer heads
   "Accessories", the one the accessory rate card prices — never saw the units. So P01 read "Accessories: Fridge"
   (the schedule's unnumbered word) with the three numbered things he typed sitting in another list further down.

   And a number that reached a reference through the supplied-numbers record became a BUILDING: WC05's committed
   supplied number is 1328978, which his own unit record says is the waste tank — so the page offered install,
   levelling and stairs on a waste tank as if it were a second toilet block. P37's retired number did the same
   through another door.

   THE RULE. A thing inside a building is contents, whichever form it came in by: a unit whose name is a contents
   type (fridge, air conditioner, urn, boiler…) is folded into the contents list. Each physical thing is listed
   ONCE: a numbered thing that matches a numbered accessory is that accessory; a numbered thing of a type the
   schedule lists without a number IDENTIFIES the schedule's one (the schedule lists a fridge, he gave the fridge as
   1328956, so that is the fridge) rather than standing beside it as a second. And a building's numbers are only
