   and the same for the steps.

   A tick per reference per item type per labour line. Unticked charges nothing and says "not ticked" — never
   nought, because a labour charge nobody has recorded is unknown, not zero. The rate is the card's and the
   quantity is the reference's, so ticking Install on a reference carrying four 6m buildings charges four
   installs. Where the card writes "Included" or "N/A" there is nothing to tick, and the page says which. */
/* a charge line's quantity as a number, or null. Number(null) is 0 and Number('') is 0, and a labour line priced at
   rate × 0 would read as a charge of nothing rather than a charge nobody can work out — so blank is null here. */
function qtyOf(l){
  const q = l && l.quantity;
  if (q == null || String(q).trim() === '') return null;
  const n = Number(q);
  return Number.isFinite(n) ? n : null;
}
/* v5.55 — A TICK PER BUILDING, WHERE THERE IS MORE THAN ONE. Andrew Fisher, 18 Sep 2026: where a location has two
   buildings, each carries its own install, levelling, steps and the rest — toilet building, toilet, container,
   sewage: two assets, priced one each.
   Said again on 19 Sep, because only the pins half of it was built the first time.

   Four references carry two asset numbers each — WC15, WC16, WC17 and WC20, every one a 6 m toilet block — and
   a single tick could not say that one of them was levelled and the other was not. The key now carries the
   asset number the same way a pin does (`fixKey`): "WC15/u1327226|Toilets & amenities|Toilet Block 6m|levelling".

   A tick recorded before this, against the reference itself, still counts — it reads as every building on that
   reference, so nothing anybody has already recorded is lost or silently changed. */
function labourKey(ref, disc, item, line, unit){
